## Landing

How `/ship-plan` lands a reviewed plan. `integrate`: `pull-request` pushes the plan's branch and opens a pull request; `direct` pushes to the default branch. `release`: `none`; `tag` tags the plan's `Release:` version, or the next free one when another plan landed it first; `tag+release` also publishes a release from that version's section of `changelog`. `changelog`: the file every plan's last step writes, in the Keep a Changelog format, `CHANGELOG.md` by default. `draft`: `no`; `yes` opens every pull request as a draft, as `/ship-plan draft` does. `follow-ups`: `issues` files a plan's follow-ups as issues in this repository; `<owner/repo>` files them in that repository; `none` files none, and `/ship-plan` lists them for you to record. `follow-up-label`: the label those issues get, `follow-up` by default; write a name with spaces as it is, such as `tech debt`. `gate`: `none`; a command, such as `./build/dod.ps1 -Fast -Affected`, that `/ship-plan` and `/run-issues` run from the repository root before each push, stopping when it exits non-zero or changes a file. `protect`: `none`; `hook` makes `.githooks/pre-push` refuse any push to the default branch, for a host that can't require pull requests, such as a private repository on GitHub Free. `pr-format`: `none`; `<file>#<heading>`, such as `AGENTS.md#Pull requests`, names the section that sets the title and description of the pull requests `/ship-plan` opens, which still end with their `Closes` lines. `review-bots`: the review bots, by login and separated by commas, whose findings `/run-issues` and `/address-feedback` may fix, `ncode-review` by default, or `none`; `/run-issues` acts only on NCode Review's `blocker` and `should-fix` findings, and neither ever fixes a bot's finding on CI, the template, agent configuration, the build or the dependencies. `max-review-rounds`: `3`; after that many rounds of review feedback on a pull request, `/run-issues` recommends the merge and leaves the bots' further findings as follow-ups. `prelude`: `none`; one PowerShell command that sets up the shell the workflow's commands run in, such as `./build/env.ps1`, a script in the repository, or `nvs use 26`, to put a tool installed through a version manager on `PATH`. `/ship-plan` and `/run-issues` run it before the gate, and executors and coordinators before each Done-when command, dot-sourced in the same PowerShell; a plan names it under Context for the executor, so no step repeats it. When it fails, they stop and show its error. Write it on one line, without a leading `. ` or `& ` operator, a `#` comment, or `;`, `|`, `&`, `&&` or `||`; `.template/Get-PlanState.ps1` prints any other value as `prelude=invalid`, and `/ship-plan` stops on it. `/run-issues` runs only when the host requires pull requests into the default branch, or `protect` is `hook` and the hooks are enabled.

- integrate: pull-request
- release: none
- changelog: CHANGELOG.md
- draft: no
- follow-ups: issues
- follow-up-label: follow-up
- gate: none
- protect: none
- pr-format: none
- review-bots: ncode-review
- max-review-rounds: 3
- prelude: none

